Deploy-time headers (not made by this repository)

Content-Security-Policy (from stage 2)

  A Web Worker takes its Content-Security-Policy from its own response
  headers, not from the page's meta tag. For the decoder worker to compile
  its WebAssembly, check.stormberry.as must send a policy that allows
  'wasm-unsafe-eval'; the zone-wide header does not. Recommended: a
  Cloudflare Response Header Transform rule scoped to check.stormberry.as,
  action Set, header Content-Security-Policy:

    default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'none'; worker-src 'self'; form-action 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

  That is the page's own meta policy plus the two directives a meta tag
  cannot carry, so the worker gets connect-src 'none' too. The minimum that
  works is the zone policy with 'wasm-unsafe-eval' added to script-src.
  tests/serve.py sends the recommended header; `python3 tests/serve.py
  --zone-csp` shows the site without it (links work, pictures say the
  reader cannot start). The onion mirror must send the same recommended
  header, in full: a policy that only adds 'wasm-unsafe-eval' lets the
  worker compile its decoder but leaves out connect-src 'none', and the
  worker's own lockdown (src/worker-lockdown.js) cannot take import() away;
  only the header stops it.

  Where the rule sits: Cloudflare runs the zone's response header rules in
  order, and when two rules set the same header the later one wins. Place
  this rule below the zone's own Content-Security-Policy rule (as the
  CheapBeer exception is), or the zone's policy replaces it.

Permissions-Policy (from stage 3, for Scan with camera)

  The zone sends Permissions-Policy with camera=(), which Chromium browsers
  (Chrome, Edge, Vanadium, Samsung Internet, Brave) obey: the camera is then
  off for every page on the zone, and Scan with camera says "This site does
  not allow the camera at the moment" without asking for it. Firefox and
  LibreWolf do not read this header, so a test in those alone would miss
  it. check.stormberry.as needs a host-scoped Cloudflare Response Header
  Transform rule, action Set, header Permissions-Policy, with the zone's
  own value and camera=(self) in place of camera=():

    geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(self), payment=(), usb=(), serial=(), bluetooth=(), accelerometer=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), interest-cohort=(), browsing-topics=(), join-ad-interest-group=(), run-ad-auction=(), private-state-token-redemption=(), private-state-token-issuance=(), attribution-reporting=()

  camera=(self) lets this site's own pages ask for the camera, and nothing
  else (no frame from another site). The browser still asks the person
  first, and the page asks only when Scan with camera is pressed. Nothing
  else in the policy changes; microphone stays off. tests/serve.py sends
  this header; `python3 tests/serve.py --zone-permissions` shows the site
  without it. The onion mirror must send the same value, or no
  Permissions-Policy at all (the browser's default for the camera is the
  site itself). The camera also needs a secure page (https, or an onion
  address in Tor Browser); over plain http the button is not shown.

  Where the rule sits matters here too, and more: later rules overwrite
  earlier ones that set the same header. The zone's own Permissions-Policy
  rule, which sends camera=() to every request, comes after the two CSP
  rules (third of seven in the list as recorded on 1 August 2026). Place
  this rule below the zone's own Permissions-Policy rule, or leave
  check.stormberry.as out of that zone rule's expression. A rule placed
  beside the CheapBeer CSP exception, above the zone's Permissions-Policy
  rule, is overwritten: nothing leaks, but Scan with camera stays blocked.
  One rule below both zone rules can set both headers for this host. After
  the change, check that the live site sends camera=(self):

    curl -sI https://check.stormberry.as/ | grep -i permissions-policy
